[{:db/id "q-333077/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "9c55f7c2-99d7-5e8e-b8fa-9a8e94964339" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333077/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "036048a7-fffe-56b1-9c1d-83abd77ebcbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333077/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-333077/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333077/field-2/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "2f81b0a8-8d61-55b9-8f17-f63e1bb5ca28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333077/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "89aa4fc8-8e69-5ebf-b786-674c7f7bbe99" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333077/field-2/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-333077/field-2/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333077/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "03ca479a-bd6b-5d66-8fbc-1371f817d0b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333077/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "01c7a0d3-c2ed-57b9-b3b9-754ab93e0df4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333077/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-333077/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333077" :question/id #uuid "577f26d2-a44a-514d-9b99-0e82cbf0597b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333077" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333077/field-1" "q-333077/field-2" "q-333077/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333077"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d7572d2f-bdce-50c3-8183-e557af4b95ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "6a26da6e-1450-5bfc-9239-65f00364dad2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef44a08-7854-5d27-b698-b03a82142141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333076/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-333076/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-333076/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-2/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "8e26bdeb-d288-525a-baf5-035c017eb653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcc4e2e-8833-5e2d-b373-9b0ba49495f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333076/field-2/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-333076/field-2/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "caa61922-c71d-57ce-9ee2-3b14f3fe01eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "724a2ded-d59e-5850-bb76-a76a25135489" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333076/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-333076/field-3/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333076" :question/id #uuid "8ba6b5e8-0a8b-5345-a03d-fa4efd887be4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333076" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y-2,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{5}{4})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333076/field-1" "q-333076/field-2" "q-333076/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333076"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-329474/field-1/df78ed3595aa493f54f4470c09f00753d8a5b28fabf1c1c57abf2bceb2f4854d" :answer/id #uuid "15d4c876-aa45-54c3-83a0-f8f80a8ceef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220142 :answer-field/correct "q-329474/field-1/df78ed3595aa493f54f4470c09f00753d8a5b28fabf1c1c57abf2bceb2f4854d" :answer-field/choices ["q-329474/field-1/df78ed3595aa493f54f4470c09f00753d8a5b28fabf1c1c57abf2bceb2f4854d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-329474/field-2/252ffbf0221558d9ad346b80ff3388aca98b65d4a4ca4a158d3b3910b019f81a" :answer/id #uuid "92ac925b-df32-5425-b6e2-71a79bb8153b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{29}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220143 :answer-field/correct "q-329474/field-2/252ffbf0221558d9ad346b80ff3388aca98b65d4a4ca4a158d3b3910b019f81a" :answer-field/choices ["q-329474/field-2/252ffbf0221558d9ad346b80ff3388aca98b65d4a4ca4a158d3b3910b019f81a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-329474/field-3/db982ba9d3f01b3a592fd3f1756168a817e4ab758e69fabf7595f3029bf3f8ec" :answer/id #uuid "126a9459-07c2-5be6-b057-e29d67efeeea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{137}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220144 :answer-field/correct "q-329474/field-3/db982ba9d3f01b3a592fd3f1756168a817e4ab758e69fabf7595f3029bf3f8ec" :answer-field/choices ["q-329474/field-3/db982ba9d3f01b3a592fd3f1756168a817e4ab758e69fabf7595f3029bf3f8ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-329474"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y+6,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-3y+6 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-3\\cdot \\frac{27}{16}+6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{29}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{16}+\\frac{29}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{137}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ | $\\frac{137}{64}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333066/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "3d6f995d-b827-58e3-be5b-f549003f0de0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333066/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "76d126c9-2db0-5a7e-bcde-8588b170ae68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220313 :answer-field/correct "q-333066/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-333066/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-333066/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333066/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "59ac2f38-6795-5380-bcfc-ce05b304e5b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220314 :answer-field/correct "q-333066/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-333066/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333066/field-3/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "66f5f057-ee70-5bf5-a77d-b0dc14c24f4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220312 :answer-field/correct "q-333066/field-3/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-333066/field-3/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333066"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-1,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4})-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056/field-1/2ebc133599183e5fdb5b6248a8ea00bdd0440ff336252ee383591a8699a758ca" :answer/id #uuid "7e2cd23e-60c1-5302-916d-36fc599ef7c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5fd95a8-b9d5-5a08-81d4-5d8db27d8d39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333056/field-1/2ebc133599183e5fdb5b6248a8ea00bdd0440ff336252ee383591a8699a758ca" :answer-field/choices ["q-333056/field-1/2ebc133599183e5fdb5b6248a8ea00bdd0440ff336252ee383591a8699a758ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "6cc7b9a3-8ede-54ed-ae6e-cb02ac3f964d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b9bbbf5-1ac8-5ed7-8f10-ff048de50b48" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333056/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-333056/field-2/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056/field-3/0e689252fcceae21513f24f0b98c844dc63de84eda7f67a46c7530656b17a5fe" :answer/id #uuid "ba04c754-68ca-5a8e-833e-7fe608531184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "69b70763-558f-5e3d-9bbc-bea7be775724" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333056/field-3/0e689252fcceae21513f24f0b98c844dc63de84eda7f67a46c7530656b17a5fe" :answer-field/choices ["q-333056/field-3/0e689252fcceae21513f24f0b98c844dc63de84eda7f67a46c7530656b17a5fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333056" :question/id #uuid "dbfab722-fb3b-5ab5-a493-ddb2d3347ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333056" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-4\\cdot (\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-6.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})-(-6))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{17}{12}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333056/field-1" "q-333056/field-2" "q-333056/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333056"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333083/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "e61e20e8-c0b3-5ea1-9796-547a3124e777" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333083/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "e7ab4993-1ff7-5e3f-b820-f94043573b37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333083/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e16b4f9f-4d92-527f-a221-16ebb688af96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333083/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-333083/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-333083/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333083" :question/id #uuid "8e5cae5a-f146-5d5e-adb7-564f362e07d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333083" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333083/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333083"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333091/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "d83a40d4-494b-5547-a01b-a980c417b217" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333091/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "49b304a1-5d3b-5c4c-957b-f6031e9561b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333091/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-333091/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333091" :question/id #uuid "85bf0736-c7b3-54bb-8d93-b3d11c3a1f60" :question/math-academy-id "q-333091" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333091/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333091"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333094/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "a8ccc663-9f8d-5788-8597-838ed2d8f764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333094/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "0635655c-0d8b-55ad-9137-b66e4c9c80f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "