nd row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ is ${y}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=2.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333093/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "b6d9e459-b254-5d1a-9b4c-ef1f7b719a69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333093/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b391edb-471e-5f0b-99df-076fcf70c270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333093/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer-field/choices ["q-333093/field-1/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333093" :question/id #uuid "735365a3-5503-5ec8-b36e-143bcb04821d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333093" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333093/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "083e7aa0-599b-54d8-a84f-d334e86ce3e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "a762a403-d197-59a1-84d2-1914ddfbcb19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "50f6c8bd-2521-5eef-b68c-d70fdf8d4a4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fd1af3e-e18a-5e16-9d10-20c60d40b864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333254/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-333254/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-333254/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-333254/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "268d31cc-6048-5419-8934-24645f2fa5fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "d8495850-e59e-5ac9-87c5-20dbbd166a1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4ffdf4a6-65f6-54fa-b902-629bee831195" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebeb9de-28e1-5104-b4ad-612180707ce5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333254/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-333254/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-333254/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-333254/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-3/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" :answer/id #uuid "84c50c60-1a57-5a84-ad78-8d7bfc867eb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-3/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "1a33aea7-5f7c-59b3-a9ab-87970daf35a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-3/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer/id #uuid "30f91b65-a252-59e0-9260-f0f06ec8cc8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "339312bd-0bba-56eb-8b9b-c8d92c616382" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333254/field-3/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" :answer-field/choices ["q-333254/field-3/3224ab5f6f5fb64621a714f7654c4df06661a69e7edb8f5993dcef1e46ce3dea" "q-333254/field-3/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-333254/field-3/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-4/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "2c024858-3ba6-50f7-be42-38097b1ce614" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-4/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "305c1b47-7122-5d35-bc09-21d7d6c4d9b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-4/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "a098f842-fd67-51f3-b241-fa97780ab645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "396b38cf-1b79-5eec-9868-8e55d07e4af8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333254/field-4/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-333254/field-4/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-333254/field-4/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-333254/field-4/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333254" :question/id #uuid "84686e6f-9f1c-54ca-afe8-fa392bb8e6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333254" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(x+1)(y+3),\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈-\\frac{9}{4},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{9}{4}+(-\\frac{3}{4})=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)({y}_{p}+3) \\\\  & =-(2+1)(-3+3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,-\\frac{9}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-3.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 0+8\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})-(-3))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=-\\frac{9}{4}+(-\\frac{3}{4})=-3\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333254/field-1" "q-333254/field-2" "q-333254/field-3" "q-333254/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333254"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333369/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1c5009b1-51a0-58a4-9b34-f363307631b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333369/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "c7656ea2-473d-5f55-8108-15a0f1495c1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333369/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "bdc51d89-2cf3-52f3-8f56-83e3d7736563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333369/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7807017-c9a0-5d74-bd79-89d785ffc902" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333369/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-333369/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-333369/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-333369/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-fiel