49c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-333369/field-6/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-333369/field-6/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-333369/field-6/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333369" :question/id #uuid "039d41c3-4c93-5f95-9d11-a88a48107cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333369" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1-(x-y)^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(2)≈2.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-(x-y)^{2} \\\\  & =1-(2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =1-({x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p})^{2} \\\\  & =1-(3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=2+1=3\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $3$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333369/field-1" "q-333369/field-2" "q-333369/field-3" "q-333369/field-4" "q-333369/field-5" "q-333369/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333253/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "8e341179-436c-50be-9872-6e1ed17af781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333253/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9