790debcf90e8fb32df14aa55ed61dab8df0566235" :answer/id #uuid "1c805b69-8184-5e0b-8106-7ec0a146e437" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{43}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333375/field-6/e013c94c0eca6959919fe5870122be0fbec23e222e3cc513b0bedda83653ae94" :answer/id #uuid "6c2369c3-4534-5fe0-ac9f-b09d39b72503" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{39}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333375/field-6" :answer-field/id #uuid "f355b140-61f4-5fd0-be8d-05dda5a120ee" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333375/field-6/3d3caea2ba50ea0ef404c4a9e8872eb86cf943f75e4987d230896ff63e8c1e9a" :answer-field/choices ["q-333375/field-6/3d3caea2ba50ea0ef404c4a9e8872eb86cf943f75e4987d230896ff63e8c1e9a" "q-333375/field-6/94fb37a1f6d80f3b2cd5649790debcf90e8fb32df14aa55ed61dab8df0566235" "q-333375/field-6/e013c94c0eca6959919fe5870122be0fbec23e222e3cc513b0bedda83653ae94"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333375" :question/id #uuid "bf4e7763-90ca-5b57-9b3f-85f8d450ddda" :question/math-academy-id "q-333375" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =3({2}^{2})-2(\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=3.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{21}{4}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3({3}^{2})-2(9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 9+8\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 78 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{13}{2}=\\frac{41}{4}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{41}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{41}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333375/field-1" "q-333375/field-2" "q-333375/field-3" "q-333375/field-4" "q-333375/field-5" "q-333375/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333375"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858/selection/f2288f9352f7ca2a51938247a89477aea196296ae7b20690ce35ebb30dfe5331" :answer/id #uuid "28ab0810-fa6e-5407-90bc-55bead537dbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858/selection/d725a8c3c7cd22443c218b0115bebaeecc3900216b2bd69742f22f0f6da95a6f" :answer/id #uuid "8c885c2e-cf09-5695-9806-de68bc86e05c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858/selection/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer/id #uuid "5cff625b-6d1d-5a50-ae70-80878d4bece2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858/selection/c936f526c80adfacd711df9decbe5d1ec9dbea02ce1693ae73b390e483368953" :answer/id #uuid "29079e76-1c26-5099-a7d5-28069725d420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858/selection/43b49f942053318d4da6be2ff054765229c5ed3de5448117844cfdcb87dc6392" :answer/id #uuid "78dea22d-bfa7-5c30-b6cc-48d3697fa7ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "640a26f9-f3c7-5e9e-b0b6-ff7bf7e1eacc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332858/selection/c936f526c80adfacd711df9decbe5d1ec9dbea02ce1693ae73b390e483368953" :answer-field/choices ["q-332858/selection/f2288f9352f7ca2a51938247a89477aea196296ae7b20690ce35ebb30dfe5331" "q-332858/selection/d725a8c3c7cd22443c218b0115bebaeecc3900216b2bd69742f22f0f6da95a6f" "q-332858/selection/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" "q-332858/selection/c936f526c80adfacd711df9decbe5d1ec9dbea02ce1693ae73b390e483368953" "q-332858/selection/43b49f942053318d4da6be2ff054765229c5ed3de5448117844cfdcb87dc6392"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332858" :question/id #uuid "7c44a114-017f-5e5a-81cd-8ca1f34c85cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332858" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y-1,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{2}{16}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{15}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-1 \\\\  & =2-\\frac{15}{16}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{21}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{7}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{64}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(2)≈\\frac{15}{64}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332858/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854/selection/e8848fe5d0660539ac00980fd60f26157ade22a1b2e19e6bf15f8c2ed31ee028" :answer/id #uuid "106ef08f-48d1-52dc-b8f5-3a1dfed7bdb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{92}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854/selection/f18416f35432d431c72acfc39d7e01add033f359c3a10a94fc360561f773edf6" :answer/id #uuid "c9f7f42e-a8db-59b3-8171-74cbc4b9db04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "53" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "0086e274-0341-5f26-8025-129f0b506a40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854/selection/1596c1b9679bfefff6b2f7ca7fe42e8769ec9be257ea5ced10273bb79ea7dc84" :answer/id #uuid "92f3f247-2671-54b0-bd6f-2ee7b9f400ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{110}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "1326fb2a-cebe-50da-8027-b94690eea5ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f55dc629-141a-523a-b54d-e28e7d1886cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332854/selection/1596c1b9679bfefff6b2f7ca7fe42e8769ec9be257ea5ced10273bb79ea7dc84" :answer-field/choices ["q-332854/selection/e8848fe5d0660539ac00980fd60f26157ade22a1b2e19e6bf15f8c2ed31ee028" "q-332854/selection/f18416f35432d431c72acfc39d7e01add033f359c3a10a94fc360561f773edf6" "q-332854/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-332854/selection/1596c1b9679bfefff6b2f7ca7fe42e8769ec9be257ea5ced10273bb79ea7dc84" "q-332854/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332854" :question/id #uuid "30b4ad39-4fd5-5a3d-94e7-8aa42628b59f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332854" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+2y-1,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈6.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2y-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 6-1 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 13-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=6+19=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{p}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 25-1 \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ | $53$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 53+8\\cdot 13-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 368 \\\\  & =\\frac{92}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ | $53$ | $\\frac{92}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =6+\\frac{92}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ | $13$ | $19$ | $25$ | $53$ | $\\frac{92}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{110}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{110}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332854/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332854"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689/selection/10a16995d4400019fd6fb7657f0b596091365fdcd33291b0e6219ab7c3a2ea00" :answer/id #uuid "cd7b6af5-217d-51d2-97a5-612a3a967685" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "46" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" :answer/id #uuid "dca8949b-2de2-5af0-bccf-f15c81934afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "dc51e2af-69ed-578a-a3f2-914b095bacae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689/selection/ca57840994d3e87d6483f3803636aa72d6493d3c1337dee7f5cc7434d25e96a3" :answer/id #uuid "857476d6-4a6a-5e64-8a00-dc77240a8533" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{49}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689/selection/f70b243a79f7565451b51a30aa09b35a857f215546db282029bd48de7b7b646d" :answer/id #uuid "0c1a849c-b768-52e3-9fa9-9e7b933ff4a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a152f82-3967-5c21-9fec-bb161caf1337" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333689/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" :answer-field/choices ["q-333689/selection/10a16995d4400019fd6fb7657f0b596091365fdcd33291b0e6219ab7c3a2ea00" "q-333689/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" "q-333689/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-333689/selection/ca57840994d3e87d6483f3803636aa72d6493d3c1337dee7f5cc7434d25e96a3" "q-333689/selection/f70b243a79f7565451b51a30aa09b35a857f215546db282029bd48de7b7b646d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333689" :question/id #uuid "f8e82999-3371-5a6b-a91a-9ab1a58e313a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333689" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈2\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =3{x}_{-1}-4{y}_{-1}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-8)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}-4{y}_{p}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-6)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{59}{2}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{32}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333689/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686/selection/fa1641b1debaf6175635ce0329c6afb9016124ed34b35c649ad25fd6a5664915" :answer/id #uuid "9ee4ba2b-d426-5770-8189-f7a523036388" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{69}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686/selection/080ef522beb9c1b2fd2988e939bb2526a1996a12823b3f69cc367c0c94afb180" :answer/id #uuid "1b857d5b-fa88-55c0-9739-710156523f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{73}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686/selection/7707786bd9d40570108f15152406f62a684ca35aa89679de28d830bf20419a05" :answer/id #uuid "8774846d-983a-5d49-b8a6-1e6e190a8aa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{67}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686/selection/5bd05cb2e10f5609fec7114f3c53d9b335cfc1e4dfba4aad99542df623bd6af2" :answer/id #uuid "8cca38b4-381a-5124-aa39-c0a021297eb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{75}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686/selection/e40585d5ae8850e94599519538efe1e780823fe17696389aa84de2b93fcef8ab" :answer/id #uuid "95be81f6-cbe1-5492-92b4-c3276096dd85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{71}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "527d6df4-b5f0-5f2e-8a38-9932b5908545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333686/selection/e40585d5ae8850e94599519538efe1e780823fe17696389aa84de2b93fcef8ab" :answer-field/choices ["q-333686/selection/fa1641b1debaf6175635ce0329c6afb9016124ed34b35c649ad25fd6a5664915" "q-333686/selection/080ef522beb9c1b2fd2988e939bb2526a1996a12823b3f69cc367c0c94afb180" "q-333686/selection/7707786bd9d40570108f15152406f62a684ca35aa89679de28d830bf20419a05" "q-333686/selection/5bd05cb2e10f5609fec7114f3c53d9b335cfc1e4dfba4aad99542df623bd6af2" "q-333686/selection/e40585d5ae8850e94599519538efe1e780823fe17696389aa84de2b93fcef8ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333686" :question/id #uuid "86981e18-3dad-5ccc-bfe2-8e7ddf358746" :question/math-academy-id "q-333686" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6x-2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =6{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{7}{3})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x-2y \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{7}{3}+3=\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =6{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{58}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{29}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{71}{18}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{71}{18}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333686/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333686"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-329310"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3+x-2{x}^{2}y,\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3+x-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =3+0-2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3+1-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{43}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{31}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{3})+8\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{83}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{83}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+(-\\frac{83}{36}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{13}{36}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(1)≈\\frac{13}{36}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21539"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x - 2y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14,$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21565"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y\\left(-2\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1.$\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21538"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x-y \\\\  & =-0-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{7}{8})=-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-1-(-\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{39}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21566"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x - 2y + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 3\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of $y'$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = (0,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}+6 \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot 2+6 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x, y) = (1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-2y+6 \\\\  & =1-2\\cdot 3+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}+6 \\\\  & =2-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+\\frac{11}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac{47}{12}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)\\approx\\dfrac{47}{12}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
